{
  "dom_id": "step-q80381",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14255877/assets/6ec5069ef688229dd8cab73ffd7110a30a25c1d3d81b29e8d35d38f75149de21.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[1,\\infty )\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. $f(1)\\ne f(3)$\n\nII. $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [1,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n\nIII. $f$ is **one-to-one**",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the graph, we have $f(1)=1$ and $f(3)<1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[1,\\infty )$ that map to the same element in $(-\\infty ,1].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[1,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
